{"A":"Research a faster, general-purpose byte-radix path building on the best measured candidate (0.01107 s). Reduce memory traffic or skip provably uniform byte passes where beneficial, while preserving strcmp ordering for every valid record, including duplicates and variable-length strings supported by the format. Audit buffer ownership and output; benchmark rather than assuming these changes help.","B":"Investigate buffered input and output to reduce the cost of reading tokens with fscanf and printing each result with printf. Preserve the required textual output and handle all valid sizes and string lengths safely. Measure end-to-end runtime alongside sorting, since the fastest measured sort may leave I/O as a substantial fraction of runtime.","C":"Explore an independent adaptive sorting design, such as MSD or hybrid radix sorting, that can avoid unnecessary full-record passes while preserving lexicographic ordering for all valid inputs. Compare it with the measured radix and qsort alternatives. OpenMP variants measured 0.01165–0.01231 s versus 0.01107 s for the best candidate, so test whether parallelism pays on the four-core target rather than assuming it does.","G":"Integrate only tree-produced changes that pass correctness checks against the oracle, and compare complete end-to-end runs. The baseline is correct at 0.41485 s; measured candidates range from 0.01107 s (best) to 0.01264 s, with no guarantee that their gains combine. Validate the integrated result across valid inputs, retain general behavior, and do not specialize to benchmark values or change tests, oracle, compiler, budgets, target, permissions, or source files outside the integration task.","analysis":"The task-relevant source and official specification are for Problem D, Bucketsort. The baseline takes 0.41485 s; the best supplied measurement is the stable LSD counting-pass candidate at 0.01107 s. qsort and OpenMP alternatives are slower in the supplied measurements (0.01165–0.01264 s), so research should prioritize the measured radix path while still testing alternatives and I/O. The measurements are not proof that candidate variants are correct or that combined changes will improve performance; correctness and full-program timing must decide integration."}